From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/a6xx: use GMU wrapper funcs for the SM6375 A619

The A619 in SM6375 is the "holi" variant, which has no real GMU: the
vendor device tree describes it as "qcom,adreno-gpu-a619-holi" with a
"gmu_wrapper" register range instead of a GMU, and there are no GMU PDC
addresses for it.

msm/adreno already knows about this GPU (adreno_is_a619_holi() gates five
register level quirks), and it decides between a real GMU and the wrapper
from the device tree:

	adreno_gpu->gmu_is_wrapper =
		of_device_is_compatible(node, "qcom,adreno-gmu-wrapper");
	...
	if (adreno_has_gmu_wrapper(adreno_gpu))
		ret = a6xx_gmu_wrapper_init(a6xx_gpu, node);
	else
		ret = a6xx_gmu_init(a6xx_gpu, node);

but the function table comes from the catalog and is never reconciled with
that decision:

	ret = adreno_gpu_init(dev, pdev, adreno_gpu, info->funcs, nr_rings);

The SM6375 entry asks for &a6xx_gpu_funcs, whose .pm_resume is
a6xx_gmu_pm_resume. So with a wrapper in the device tree the GPU is set up
as a wrapper but then resumed as if it had a real GMU, and the SoC hangs
hard on the first runtime resume, before a6xx_pm_resume() is ever reached.
On this phone that means an unrecoverable boot loop.

This went unnoticed because no upstream device tree enables the SM6375 GPU,
so the entry could not be exercised.

Point the entry at &a6xx_gmuwrapper_funcs, matching the A610 and A702
wrapper entries, and drop the GMU firmware, which a wrapper never loads.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_catalog.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_catalog.c
@@ -840,12 +840,11 @@
 		.revn = 619,
 		.fw = {
 			[ADRENO_FW_SQE] = "a630_sqe.fw",
-			[ADRENO_FW_GMU] = "a619_gmu.bin",
 		},
 		.gmem = SZ_512K,
 		.quirks = ADRENO_QUIRK_4GB_VA,
 		.inactive_period = DRM_MSM_INACTIVE_PERIOD,
-		.funcs = &a6xx_gpu_funcs,
+		.funcs = &a6xx_gmuwrapper_funcs,
 		.zapfw = "a615_zap.mdt",
 		.a6xx = &(const struct a6xx_info) {
 			.hwcg = a615_hwcg,
